-- title:
--   Lemma A.1, p.13 — the unipotent elements of a solvable $\mathcal G^\circ$ are the closure of the group generated by the $g_u$, $g\in\Delta$
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal G\le GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ be a Zariski-closed subgroup whose identity component $\mathcal G^\circ$ is solvable, and let $\Delta\subseteq\mathcal G^\circ$ be a Zariski-dense subgroup. Then the unipotent elements of $\mathcal G^\circ$ form a group $\mathcal U$, and
--   $$\mathcal U=\overline{\langle g_u:g\in\Delta\rangle}^{\,\mathrm{Zar}} .$$
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - encoding: "$\mathcal G^\circ$ solvable" → $\mathcal G^\circ$ given as a subgroup (hypothesis `hG0`, fact (A1)) that is solvable; reason: (A1) makes the identity component of any closed subgroup a subgroup, so the hypothesis excludes nothing
--   - encoding: the unipotent Jordan part $g_u$ → a relation: $u$ is unipotent, commutes with a semisimple $s$, and $g=su$; reason: the Jordan decomposition is unique, so for invertible $g$ exactly one $u$ qualifies
--   - encoding: Zariski closure via polynomials in the entries and $\det^{-1}$ → polynomials in the entries, closed sets taken relative to $GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$; reason: multiplying by a power of $\det$, a unit on $GL$, gives the same closed subsets of $GL$
--   - encoding: identity component $\mathcal G^\circ$ of the algebraic group → the union of all Zariski-connected subsets of $\mathcal G$ containing $1$ (the connected component of $1$); reason: this is the identity component of a linear algebraic group; its group and finite-index properties (A1) are theorems, not definitions
--   - generalization: $\mathcal G$ the Zariski closure of a group $\Gamma\le GL_d(\mathbb Q)$ (§2.3 notation) → any Zariski-closed subgroup $\mathcal G$ of $GL_d(\overline{\mathbb Q})$; reason: the proof (Borel 10.6, Lie–Kolchin, (A2)) holds for every linear algebraic group over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$; the paper's $\mathcal G$ is a special case
-- source:
--   Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups, STOC 2027 submission (manuscript sha256 434d3094df2665371c402d5b5291b3c7f3a27a0d79384e4a8ec3ca3a46a92543), Lemma A.1, p.13 (PDF p.15)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_CopylessCRA_zariskiClosure

namespace CopylessCRA

/-- **Lemma A.1.** Let `𝒢°` be solvable and let `∆ ⊆ 𝒢°` be a Zariski dense subgroup. Then the group
`𝒰` of unipotent elements of `𝒢°` is the Zariski closure of the group generated by `{g_u : g ∈ ∆}`.

Zeng–Cao–Dai–Wang–Ding, *Copyless Cost Register Automata and Algebraic Groups*, STOC 2027
submission (manuscript sha256 434d3094…), Lemma A.1, p.13 (PDF p.15).

**Formalization Note** `𝒢` is any Zariski-closed subgroup of `GL_d(ℚ̄)` and `𝒢°` its identity
component; the hypothesis `hG0` names `𝒢°` as a subgroup (fact (A1), p.6, true for every closed
subgroup) so that "solvable" can be stated. `g_u` is the unipotent Jordan part over `ℚ̄`
(`IsUnipotentJordanPart`). The conclusion includes that the unipotent elements of `𝒢°` form a
subgroup. -/
theorem lemma_A_1 {d : ℕ} (G : Subgroup (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ)
    (hG : IsZariskiClosed (G : Set (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ))
    (G0 : Subgroup (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ)
    (hG0 : (G0 : Set (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ) = identityComponent (G : Set _))
    (hsolv : Group.IsSolvable G0) (Δ : Subgroup (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ) (hΔ : Δ ≤ G0)
    (hdense : zariskiClosure (Δ : Set (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ) = G0) :
    (∃ U : Subgroup (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ, (U : Set (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ) =
      {g | g ∈ G0 ∧ IsUnipotent (g : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)}) ∧
    {g | g ∈ G0 ∧ IsUnipotent (g : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)} =
      zariskiClosure (Subgroup.closure {u : (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ | ∃ g ∈ Δ,
        IsUnipotentJordanPart (g : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar) (u : Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)} :
          Set (Matrix (Fin d) (Fin d) Qbar)ˣ) := by sorry

end CopylessCRA
